it is great to welcome back to the  program richard wolff  professor emeritus of economics at my  alma mater the university of  massachusetts he taught me  introductory macroeconomics a long long  time ago  currently a visiting professor at new  school university in new york city the  new book  is the sickness is the system when  capitalism fails to save us from  pandemics or itself and you you make the  point in the book that i've been making  for some time which is  that the pandemic exposed the inherent  fragility  of many systems which we take for  granted this includes  the streams of income from our jobs  whatever they may be  this includes the food supply chain as  we very quickly saw in grocery stores  toilet paper the inability of government  our government others have moved far  more quickly but the inability of our  government to quickly react to changing  circumstances  give us the kind of big picture of what  what the takeaway should be  well i think it's a stark illustration  of something which  has been difficult for many americans to  get their  their heads around so let me be blunt  and make the point that the  the book makes uh in a variety of ways  if you think about it we all know that  we had a terrible virus back in 1918  killed 600 000 americans we know very  well how damaging viruses  are in recent years we have studied  murs sars ebola i mean  there is no excuse for a modern country  let alone a rich one let alone a an  economy with a well-developed  medical practice and education system  to be unprepared for a virus there  really is  no excuse and when you remember that an  economic system  if it has any priority the public health  of the people who live in it  has to be right up there number one two  or three  why in the world were we unprepared we  had companies that can produce  masks and gloves and ventilators and all  of that  but they didn't do it and i know why i'm  an economist what i study  it isn't profitable no company wants to  produce masks  uh by the millions store them in  warehouses around the country where the  population  is concentrated make sure that they are  clean  make sure that they don't deteriorate  replace those that do  watch the whole thing and then you never  know when the next virus will be  will you have to sit on all of this for  two years for six years  for 20 years so it's not profitable it's  not a rational  capitalist profit driven calculation to  make  so the companies didn't and we didn't  have the ventilators the gloves the  masks and all the rest  and there's no shorthand way of saying  it private capitalism  and the public health don't work  well together they don't solve the  problem  okay we could have had a secondary  solution  the government could have come in and  said what i just said  private capitalism is not capable of  making us prepared for a virus  so we're going to do it we're going to  buy the masks as fast as you produce  them  ditto the ventilators ditto the gloves  and then we're gonna store them at  taxpayer expense  in fact in warehouses and distribute  them  and monitor them and clean them and  replace them we'll do all that  because private capitalism fails the  government  is going to come in and compensate but  we didn't do that either  because our government is trapped in  this ideology  that somehow private capitalism does  everything  maximally efficiently maximally it's all  wrong  well they did have now they did have the  national stockpile right i mean in a  sense that's what it is it was  completely misdistributed  and they did nothing with it for months  but to some degree isn't that what the  national stockpile is  yes and and look we have plenty of  examples of where the government  can do it the best example that i use is  defense  right you know it's not profitable to  make a missile and store it for 10 years  or a gun or a ship or a plane but we do  what happens is the government comes  right in  buys the thing as fast as the defense  companies produce it  and then the government pays to store it  monitor it and clean it  so we know how to do it but it wasn't  done because we're so ideologically  blocked  that what we can do for defense we won't  actually do for our own  personal public health we've been  cutting back  on rural hospitals we've been coming  back on that stockpile  we have now shown ourselves to have  what i mean here's the statistic that i  think ends all conversation  the united states has four and a half  percent of the world's people and we  have 25  of the world's covet cases and covet  deaths  we're a rich country that is a statistic  that screams something fundamental is  wrong if a society with our capability  has such a record so on the economic  response  which countries did best in terms of  dealing with the financial insecurity  the job loss as a result of social  distancing measures are what what are  the examples we should look to  yeah i'm afraid that there again i have  bad news but it's a bad news it's also a  wake-up call  yeah the united states did not do well  let me give you the starkest example  in the united states over the last six  months  the richest people in the country gained  tens of billions  more of their wealth those numbers are  well known i won't repeat them  over the same period of time more than  50 million people  had to file for unemployment  compensation  either by the state or the federal  government i mean a starker rendition of  inequality  you could not have at the same time we  were all telling each other  this virus is a great danger to all of  us we  all have to work hard together we will  get through this together  yeah some people got through it together  by becoming richer  and an awful lot of people are getting  through it  by becoming poorer because of  unemployment why do i stress this  in the european countries it wasn't done  unemployment in italy  during the entire covid period was less  than it was a year before that when  there was no uh  coven german unemployment went from five  percent  roughly in march to six percent roughly  now in short in every major european  country they did not do  what the united states did they didn't  throw tens of millions of people  out of work throwing them on  unemployment  adding to their losses of unemployment  the  unspeakable anxiety of wondering whether  they never have a job again  whether the job they lost would be there  when they went back  looking for it which we all know  millions of them won't we already  know that um and so they're all  wondering is the roulette wheel gonna  come down and point on me  uh to add anxiety  to the worries about the illness to the  worries about managing your children who  can't go to school  your distance i mean it's unspeakable  and let me tell you why  in europe the reaction was so much  better  the european leaders whether they're  right wing left wing or in the middle  they know the following labor unions  socialist parties communist parties  they're much stronger in europe  than they are in the united states and  that's been true for a long time  and everyone there knows if you had  dared  throw tens of millions of working people  out of their jobs  out of the security of their jobs they  wouldn't have gone home  to watch tv they would have gone into  the streets  and they would have shut down those  economies way more totally  than any pandemic could and everyone  there knew it so here was the deal in  europe the government will help you  in your business but you cannot  fire anyone you get it  that's the condition and that was  accepted in europe  and that's how they've handled it here  in the united states  we don't have the political muscle on  the left  in the labor movement to create a  comparable  awareness so the trump administration  could  go ahead the democrats provided very  little  opposition even the afl-cio  hasn't had a demonstration to say this  is taking out the difficulties of a  virus  on the mass of people who have no guilt  whatsoever why are they singled out  why is it possible to impose  unemployment on 50 million people  while the richest people gain billions i  mean this is  beyond words and my book is an attempt  to try to get at how this came about and  how it works  so that people can contextualize these  awful results  yeah the book is the sickness is the  system when capitalism fails to save us  from pandemics or itself  we're going to go to a break on the tv  and radio show with professor richard  wolff but you'll be able to find our  entire conversation which will continue  on on the youtube channel  so in in looking forward now there for  months there's been this idea of another  so-called stimulus uh there have been  discussions about  will it happen won't it happen who's for  it who's against it we're  now 38 days or however many from a  presidential election  there's been a failure to do that second  stimulus  when you look at that what does that  failure  tell you about the bigger picture of how  elected officials are seeing this  pandemic  well it tells me a very sobering thought  that the kinds of calculations the  people in power make  simply do not take into account  not only the suffering of large numbers  of people  which is itself a serious problem of  disconnection  but they don't even take into account  the economic viability of this system  it gives me this amazing sense those of  us who  know and are willing to say it  ideologically  this capitalist system in the united  states is now  on government life support that has to  be understood  a couple of examples the federal reserve  has now started  buying corporate debt directly and  indirectly  massive amounts i don't know whether  americans really understand what that  means  it means every corporation in america  that has  any kind of economic problem they're  producing something that people don't  want or their technology is out of date  or they have bad relations with their  working people  whatever it is the cheapest  easiest way to solve the problem is to  go borrow money  to throw that money at it why because  the federal reserve is  literally creating the money and  charging you virtually zero  interest rate for it and when you borrow  from a bank  the bank simply turns around and sells  that corporate debt  to the federal reserve so in the end the  creditor of the american business  community  is the united states government which is  making that money available  and which is keeping interest rates down  you are  subsidizing the entire capitalist system  and as if that weren't enough you're  also dishing out  huge amounts of money to unemployment  compensation  for workers that are out of a job to all  kinds of special benefits during the  covet period  for given loans to small businesses etc  etc this is an economy that lives off  the government  therefore you cannot cut back the  support the government gives  because that teeter-totters the system  since that's what it's sitting on  when they decided not to renew the extra  600  per week for unemployment as of the end  of july  july 31st they were removing  one key support for this  economic system and the statistics  already show  that the upswing that was supposed to be  a recovery  is now on shaky ground no surprise  there i'm struck and i have to say this  i'm struck by a system that is at once  cruel to the people who deserve it least  and self-destructive in its  loyalty to an ideology that the  government should be not  doing things that it ought to obviously  do  during this period and i take both of  those things  as signs of serious long-term  economic decline and i think that's what  is the context for all of this yeah  our our next conversation should be a  deeper dive  into what we are being told about what  the recovery will look like  and and what actually is is more likely  to happen probably  probably for our next conversation we've  been speaking with economist richard  wolff the new book  is the sickness is the system when  capitalism  fails to save us from pandemics or  itself  uh as always uh very much appreciate  your time  thank you very much david and very much  appreciate the opportunity to talk with  you about these things

